timestamp=2026-11-09T09:01:22.514Z level=INFO run=dc3d0a36 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Another row: row 304, emp 88032898468 (Natalia, rate 27). hours 4, bonus 760, salary_amount 621.\n# 4*27+760 = 868, salary_amount is 621. 621 is less than 4*27=108. That means salary_amount is less than even base. That’s confusing.\n# But the task says \"calculate the total monthly salary...\" as defined by the formula. Maybe the timesheet’s salary_amount/bonus columns are just data in the dataset — possibly unreliable? \n# The task says \"If any invalid or inconsistent data are found in the input, they should be corrected where possible based on available data, or otherwise excluded from the final results.\"\n# So we must compute using the formula from the task description, using the data provided (with corrections). That’s key.\n# \n# So: for each employee, per month (billing period), total salary = (total hours worked in month × hourly rate) + (bonuses earned for completed tasks in that month).\n# Hourly rate comes from employees.csv. Hours worked come from timesheet rows (sum per month). Bonuses from completed tasks in that month — probably sum of pay_per_completion for tasks performed in that month? Or sum of bonus values as given?\n# The task says \"bonuses earned for completed tasks in that month\" — so bonus amounts are defined by tasks? Check tasks.csv: pay_per_completion is listed. So bonus for a completed task is pay_per_completion. So we sum pay_per_completion of all tasks completed by that employee in that month (across all their shifts in that month).\n# That’s independent of the \"bonus\" column in timesheet. The \"bonus\" column in timesheet might be precomputed or might be inconsistent. We should use the task definition.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
