timestamp=2026-11-09T09:01:35.334Z level=INFO run=dc3d0a36 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Now we need to determine the billing period/month grouping.\n# \"For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record.\n# On that day, employees are required to appear at the workplace to collect their salary.\"\n# \n# So months are not calendar months? The billing month is defined by billing periods. The billing period starts on the same day of the week as the dataset's earliest record. And each billing month is such a period (or periods form months in that billing cycle)? \n# Or more concretely: find the earliest record in the dataset (earliest date overall). Look at what day of week that date is. Then billing periods start on that day of week. So a \"billing month\" is a period that starts on that weekday and covers some interval (e.g., 4 weeks) — or is it aligned to calendar months but starting from that anchor? Or does \"monthly salary for each employee for every month\" refer to calendar months, and the billing period rule is about when salaries are collected (payday)? That changes grouping.\n# \n# The sentence: \"For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record. On that day, employees are required to appear at the workplace to collect their salary.\"\n# This sounds like: the billing cycle starts on a specific weekday (inherited from earliest record). So each billing period (month period) begins on that weekday. That means when we group work into \"billing months\", we group by those periods, not necessarily calendar months. That’s important.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
